\description{
Reads a variability model written in UVL, or the Boolean formula of a model
written in any other notation (a DIMACS file, such as those that KMax,
KConfigReader, KconfigSampler or TraVarT produce from Kconfig and other
languages), and computes every \emph{strong} binary relationship between its
features, that is, every relationship that holds in all the valid
configurations:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{requires}: selecting \code{A} always selects \code{B} (an arrow \code{A -> B});
\item \strong{excludes}: \code{A} and \code{B} are never selected together;
\item \strong{at least one} (with \code{or_graph = TRUE}): every valid configuration has
\code{A} or \code{B}.
}

It also finds the core features (in every product), the dead features (in
none), the atomic sets (features that are always selected together), the
minimal form of the graphs (\code{vp_minimal()}), and, with \code{kernel = TRUE}, the
kernel (\code{vp_kernel()}). The results are written as files (Pajek graphs,
lists, a CSV report and a JSON summary) into \code{output_dir}, and read back as a
model, the object that every other function of the package takes.

This is the command \verb{strong4vm generate} of the Strong4VM program; every
option of the command line is an argument. Its other sub-commands are
functions of the package: \code{check} is \code{\link[=vp_check]{vp_check()}}, \code{expand} is \code{\link[=vp_expand]{vp_expand()}},
\code{explain} is \code{\link[=vp_explain_conflict]{vp_explain_conflict()}} and \code{\link[=vp_explain_requires]{vp_explain_requires()}}, and \code{diff}
is \code{\link[=vp_compare]{vp_compare()}} and \code{\link[=vp_changed_links]{vp_changed_links()}}.
}
\section{What the graphs cannot show}{
 The graphs hold relationships between
\strong{two} features. A rule that involves three or more features at once (such
as "A requires B or C", or "A, B and C cannot all be selected") is not in
any graph, unless it implies a relationship between two features.
Everything the graphs say is true; what they do not say may still be ruled
out by such a rule. \code{representativeness = TRUE} measures how much of the
model the graphs leave out (see \code{\link[=vp_representativeness]{vp_representativeness()}}).
}

